findings.description:
The superseded probe contract had `probe_version`, a defined field set, behaviour on unknown versions, and "CF tests check the field names as a cross-repo contract" (line 135). The amendment reverses the direction: "the contract between cf and squadron is now cf's detection report and prompt delivery (slice 271), which squadron calls". But it states none of the same guarantees for the new contract:

- no version field in the report JSON;
- no outcome enum shared across repos;
- no rule for adding facts without breaking callers;
- no defined error shape when the guide prompt is missing.

Squadron will build `sq onboard` against this JSON. With slice 269 changing outcomes and partial adoption still unresolved (first finding), the schema is likely to move. Add a `report_version` and fixed outcome identifiers, and say whether cf ships a contract fixture for squadron to test against.

A slice-plan marker is text that any editor can write, so the checker cannot know who set it. What actually bounds misuse are the structural checks in `packages/core/src/introspection/retroactiveRules.ts:85-124`:

- no design or task file;
- the entry is checked;
- it is indexed below all new work, computed across *all* plans by `lowestNewWorkIndex`.

The arch mentions only the first and calls the relative order "the only thing squadron 364 relies on". It should say that the order is enforced by the checker, and enforced globally across initiatives. Otherwise slice design may pick an index scheme, such as one existing-state plan per layer at a higher index band, that the checker rejects.
